Adding a few concrete snippets and minor clarifications (see node-snippet.txt attached). Node example below (paste into README):

--- node snippet ---
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_API_KEY, timeout: 15000 });

async function callFeature(payload) {
  try {
    const res = await client.generate({ model: 'redwood-v2', input: payload });
    return res;
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.status === 429 && err.headers['retry-after']) {
      // respect server backoff
      await sleep(parseInt(err.headers['retry-after'], 10) * 1000);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
--- end snippet ---

Rate-limit handling notes:
- Prefer server-guided backoff: honor Retry-After header when present
- For 429 w/o Retry-After: use exponential backoff with full jitter and cap retries to 4 attempts
- Implement local queueing with max queue depth to avoid unbounded memory growth; return 503 to caller when queue over threshold so calling service can apply its own backpressure
- For high-volume workflows, use batching where possible (client-side aggregation of small requests) to reduce token exchange overhead

